  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 154 PM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nWednesday-Thursday...A stronger shortwave trough will swing \ninto the base of the longwave situated over the Upper \nMississippi River Valley on Wednesday, finally pushing the cold \nfront further southeast along with the scattered showers and \nstorms. Areas that receive repeated rounds of storms, especially\nafter rainfall Tuesday night, could see some localized flash \nflooding which is why the WPC has issued a Slight Risk (level 2 \nout of 4) for excessive rainfall for most of the CWA. Along and \nahead of the front on Wednesday, we may see the chance for \nstrong to severe storms as well. Overall, confidence is low, \nowing to weaker shear/instability. The SPC has highlighted areas\nalong/south of Highway 34 in a Marginal Risk (level 1 of 5) on \nWednesday. Primary hazard with any storm would be severe wind \ngusts. By Wednesday night, a high pressure shifts into the CWA \nas convergence aloft continues to dry things out. Isolated \nshowers and storms (20-30% coverage) could stick around in \nnortheast Missouri and west-central Illinois along an area of \nlow-level isentropic upglide near the baroclinic zone into \nThursday morning. \n\nFriday-Sunday...The longwave trough axis propagates into the Great \nLakes region, providing additional ageostrophic convergence aloft \nFriday into Saturday. As the high pressure over the region moves \neastward, southerly flow will likely return into the weekend and \nadvect in some warmer and more moist air. Highs could climb back \ninto the upper 80s and low 90s by Sunday (50-80% chance for 90 in \nour southwest, lower probs north, per 03.00z LREF run). \nPrecipitation chances have trended down for the weekend, with PoPs \nreturning to the area Sunday night through Monday.",
